A||||4||fierospeeder||Reclining seats Are Dangerous when vehicle is in motion!||||1||
Z||000000||fierospeeder||11-22-2000||03:43 PM||||I think it was fox news that showed this deal about reclined seats.<P>"the gov knows it and auto manufacterers now it, why dont they tell you"<P>Anyway, the story is, 2-4 highschool 19 year olds were in a car going home after a late night a few years ago.  The passenger, female, had her chair reclined all the way back and was sleeping with her seat belt on.<BR>Yes we all know thats not dangerous.<P>The driver, was counting sheep, and falls asleep behind the wheel and they smack into a tree or a pole, total front end damage.  The passenger gets injured because her belt didn't restrain her, i guess thats the idea and she suffured irreversible brain damage, shes a little bit slow now but she can walk and talk.<P>They tried to sue honda, but honda settled out of court.  The mother was bitching that the automakers should put warnings in the car so the people should be aware of it.<P>The news did say it is in the "owners manual" and states "like ya who reads a owners manual"  that the auto manufac should place warnings like air bags and blames them for this.<P>I blame the driver, one for falling asleep, and a careless driver for not reading the manual and or allowing the passenger to do that.   If your passenger doesn't wear his or her seatbelt, you are the responsible one because its your vehicle, and same should be for the reclined seat. <P>Another thing was 20/20 stating that people would overfill their SUVs and it wouldn't drive correctly because it exceeded its weight capacity, and people didn't know it cause they didn't read their owners manual.<P>I guess we should make vehicles as simple as a machintosh because IBM compatibles come with a numerous paged manual and commodores and other old computers came with a 300 page book.||||reg||1
Z||000001||fropuff||11-22-2000||05:32 PM||||You'd think that they wouldn't be able to be sued becuase it was in the manual.  With the seat reclined, the girl had to fly up many feet before hitting the belt.  Even if it was one that locked up, she still could have smacked her face on something.  It even says in the owner's manual that the seat is not supposed to be reclined with the vehicle in motion.  It's even got big pictures for those illiterate people!  Illiterates shouldn't be driving anyway, but that's another story.  Maybe they should put the owner's manual on tape.  That's an idea.<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000002||gixxer||11-22-2000||06:06 PM||||Yes, they probably didn't have a chance of taking it to court and winning. If the case is low enough profile, the company will settle out of court just to save the expense of mounting a defence. That's what the people probably wanted all along. <BR>Easy money.<BR>If the company decided to defend themselves and went to court, they could probably kick these people's butts around pretty bad and win. But it would be expensive, and they would receive some bad publicity in the bargain.<BR>It's all about easy money, not what's right.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||11-22-2000||06:46 PM||||Actually out of court settlement is often more about sealing the records of the company involved. They don't care much about the money. The insurance usually pays it.<P>They do care that documents involved in the suit become part of public court records if the case goes to trial. Non disclosure agreements are nearly always part of settlements. Firestone used this practice repeatedly on the tires now being recalled.<P>If you look at the history of the auto industry there is a repeated pattern of fighting both emission regulation and safety improvments.<P>If you look at air bags for example you'll find the majority of low speed fatalities caused by the airbag itself are in the U.S. There are a number of reasons for that but the main one is that U.S. car makers generally set the air bag deploy point lower than it should be. A simple change of the airbag deploy control would eliminate nearly all these deaths. Instead cars are still made with this design flaw and we are told to wait several more years while the air bag system is made "smart".<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||gixxer||11-22-2000||07:59 PM||||Since the company that I work for (Boeing Commercial Airplanes) is heavily regulated  by the the federal government (the FAA), the cost of assembling the relevant information (B.O.L.D., BDMs,Lessons Learned, Preliminary Design Review, Critical Design Review, Maintenence Manuals, FARs, etc, etc.)and going to court becomes more of a factor than sealing any records. The engineering information and thought processes that are documented and involved in designing an airplane, are assembled by the Company, and then distributed to the litigants laywers. If the litigant's lawyers can't get what they want from the company, they can probably get it thru the government (the FAA), the airline, or from "expert witnesses". <BR>So, much of the burden appears to be put on the company, which is time consuming, and expensive. <BR>No doubt many companies are able to "seal their records". But mine can't, at least not to any extent that could help.<BR>At any rate, in a jury trial, the lawyers tend to resort to arousing the jurers emotions to favor their clients, rather than try to deal with technical facts, which can be quickly boring. <BR>Since most jurers don't have a technical mindset, be it regarding seatbelts, tires, or airplanes, baseing a defence or procecution on technical data is probably a bad idea. <BR>However, people tend to understand a severed limb or a burned corpse very easily.<BR>  <BR>  <BR>||||reg||1
